# THE BYTES A CLONE SEES ARE THE BYTES THE PIN DESCRIBES.
#
# `contracts/shape-pin.yaml` records a sha256 of every copied shape file, and
# `scripts/validate-pins.py` recomputes each one from the bytes on disk. Git
# for Windows' installer default is `core.autocrlf=true`, so a clone there
# writes CRLF into the worktree; the digest of CRLF bytes is not the digest of
# the LF bytes the pin records, and `make validate` then goes red on EVERY
# pinned file for a colleague who did nothing but clone the repository.
#
# That is fixed here rather than on each machine because the repository is
# what knows: `* text=auto eol=lf` normalises text to LF in the object store
# on check-in and checks it out as LF on every platform, whatever
# `core.autocrlf` is set to. Nobody has to be told a git setting before
# cloning, and nobody who was never told is punished for it.
#
# NO PATH IS LISTED BY NAME, and that is a decision rather than an omission. A
# file git's heuristic read as binary would not be normalised, so a pinned
# file it misjudged would need `text eol=lf` spelled out for it. Every pinned
# file is text — YAML, Python, Markdown, a Makefile, a workflow — so there is
# nothing for the heuristic to get wrong, and `text=auto` leaves binary
# detection where it belongs: with git, for the files this project adds later.
#
# openRepoShape #51, ruled by Brett Heap on 2026-09-05.

* text=auto eol=lf
